= 13 July 2014 — The new Secretary-General

For the first time, the PSOE's members have chosen their leader directly. You, **Pedro Sánchez**, a 42-year-old economist and Madrid deputy little known outside the party, have won with 48.3% of the vote, ahead of Eduardo Madina (36.0%) and José Antonio Pérez Tapias (15.0%). You beat the polls. Almost every survey of Socialist voters had Madina ahead, by as much as thirteen points in Metroscopia's last poll for El País, and the final Sigma Dos poll still gave him a lead of three. You had toured the agrupaciones one by one, and the members gave you a twelve-point win.

{!<div id="sp-primary-2014"></div>!}

You did not win alone. Susana Díaz, President of Andalusia and the most powerful figure in the party, chose not to stand and put the Andalusian federation, the largest in the PSOE, behind you. Most of the regional barons followed. They expect a leader who will listen to them.

The country you inherit is raw. Unemployment is above 24%. Mariano Rajoy governs with an absolute majority won in 2011, and has spent it on austerity and a labour reform the unions call the hardest in democracy. Corruption cases surround the PP. In May's European elections the two big parties together fell below 50% for the first time, and a new party called Podemos, founded in January, won 8% and five seats.

The general election is due by the end of 2015. At the Extraordinary Congress in two weeks you must say what your PSOE stands for.

- @renewal: A new PSOE: renewal, clean politics, a younger face
- @left_turn: Back to the left: tax the rich, repeal the labour reform
- @unity: A party of government: unity with the federations and the old guard

@renewal
on-arrival: {! Q.opening = 1; Q.modernisers_dissent -= 5; Q.modernisers_strength += 3; Q.institutionalists_dissent += 3; if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.target(Q, 'psoe', 'young', 2); window.SpainGame.target(Q, 'psoe', 'middle', 1.5); window.SpainGame.recalculate(Q); } if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.position(Q, -0.5, 0.5); } !}

You fill the new executive with people under 45 and promise primaries, transparency and an end to the old ways. Commentators call it "the new PSOE". The old guard calls it inexperience.

@left_turn
on-arrival: {! Q.opening = 2; Q.progressives_dissent -= 8; Q.trade_unionists_dissent -= 6; Q.regional_leaders_dissent += 5; Q.institutionalists_dissent += 4; Q.rel_up += 5; if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.target(Q, 'psoe', 'working', 2.5); window.SpainGame.recalculate(Q); } if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.position(Q, 2.5, 0.5); } !}

You promise to repeal the labour reform and to reform the Constitution's article 135, the austerity amendment your own party voted for in 2011. The left of the party is surprised and pleased. Díaz's people are surprised.

@unity
on-arrival: {! Q.opening = 3; Q.regional_leaders_dissent -= 5; Q.institutionalists_dissent -= 6; Q.diaz_relation += 8; Q.sanchismo -= 3; if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.target(Q, 'psoe', 'pensioners', 2); window.SpainGame.recalculate(Q); } if (window.SpainGame) { window.SpainGame.position(Q, -1, 0); } !}

The barons get the posts they asked for. The party is calm; the voters notice little difference.
